<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red Turban Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1351–<a href="/years/1368">1368</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Red Turban Rebellion was a series of uprisings against Mongol rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, beginning in 1351 under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a> millenarian beliefs and lasting to 1368. It spread across central China and produced competing warlords. One of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, defeated his rivals, drove out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> in 1368.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion.jpg" alt="Red Turban Rebellion" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Turban.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Yuan court was split in the early 1300s between a Mongol-centric faction and one inclined to a Han-based Confucian style of government, and the last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghon-temur">Toghon Temür</a>, came to the throne as a child in 1332. The Yuan garrisons, mostly Han soldiers, were in steep decline, and by 1340 repeatedly failed to put down local rebellions. From the 1340s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> flooded constantly, and epidemics and cold weather struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. One epidemic that began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> in 1331 is said to have killed 13 million people by 1333.</p><p>The Red Turbans appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> in the 1330s, and their sects worshipped Mile Pusa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a>. The movement is traced to the monk Peng Yingyu, who led an uprising in Yuanzhou in 1338.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1351 about 150,000 farm workers were mobilised to rechannel the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and open the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, on the orders of the chancellor Toqto'a. Han Shantong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-futong">Liu Futong</a> recruited from the discontented workers. Han was captured and executed, but his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> was proclaimed the Young Prince of Radiance, claiming descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, and Liu set up a Red Turban government. In the south Peng Yingyu and Zou Pusheng enthroned the cloth peddler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-shouhui">Xu Shouhui</a> as emperor of Tianwan in 1351. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a> later murdered Xu, took the throne in 1360 and renamed the dynasty Han.</p><p>Yuan armies suppressed most of the Red Turbans by 1353, but Toqto'a was dismissed in January 1355 while campaigning against the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng">Zhang Shicheng</a>, after which the court lost control of its own forces.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, an orphan and former monk born in 1328, joined the rebels in 1352 under Guo Zixing and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in 1356. He accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> as emperor but presented himself as a Yuan subject restoring order against rebels.</p><p>In 1363 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-poyang">Battle of Lake Poyang</a>, then eliminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng">Zhang Shicheng</a>, who died in 1367, and Fang Guozhen. Han Lin'er drowned crossing the Changjiang in January 1367. Zhu proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> at Yingtian on 12 January 1368, calling for the overthrow of the barbarians and the restoration of the Chinese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The remnants of the Yuan court retreated north and are known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a>. Zhu ordered mass relocations afterwards: people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> were moved into devastated provinces in the north, and about 500,000 were settled in his hometown of Fengyang in 1367.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Red Turban Rebellions” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>But <b>Red Turban</b> rebels invaded in 1359 and 1361, briefly taking Kaegyong, Japanese pirates ravaged the coasts, and Gongmin was murdered in 1374.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseon was founded by Yi Seong-gye, a general from the northeastern frontier who had won fame fighting <b>Red Turban</b> rebels and Japanese pirates in the last decades of Goryeo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Zhu came out of the <b>Red Turban Rebellion</b> (c. 1351–1368).</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ilkhanate disintegrated after Abu Sa'id's death in 1335, and the Yuan dynasty was driven from China in 1368 by the Ming, founded by Zhu Yuanzhang, a leader who had risen in the <b>Red Turban Rebellion</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Red Turban Rebellion</b>, which broke out in 1351 under the millenarian banner of the White Lotus tradition, spread rapidly across the Huai and Yangtze valleys.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Plague and famine ravaged the Huai River lands in the 1330s, floods broke the Yellow River’s banks, and the <b>Red Turban Rebellion</b> broke out in 1351.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
